\text{Suppose there are } n \text{ identical objects, three of which are marked. A device can test any subset of the objects and return whether there are 0, 1, or more than 1 marked objects in the subset. Let } L(n) \text{ be the minimum number of tests required to identify all three marked objects.}

\text{a) Compute } L(6)\text{.}

\text{b) Show that } L(n) \leq \frac{n+4}{2}\text{.}

\text{Suppose there are } n \text{ identical objects, three of which are marked. A device can test any subset of the objects and return whether there are 0, 1, or more than 1 marked objects in the subset. Let } L(n) \text{ be the minimum number of tests required to identify all three marked objects.}

\text{a) Compute } L(6)\text{.}

\text{b) Show that } L(n) \leq \frac{n+5}{3}\text{.}

\text{Suppose there are } n \text{ identical objects, three of which are marked. A device can test any subset of the objects and return whether there are 0, 1, or more than 1 marked objects in the subset. Let } L(n) \text{ be the minimum number of tests required to identify all three marked objects.}

\text{a) Compute } L(6)\text{.}

\text{b) Show that } L(n) \geq \frac{n+5}{2}\text{.}

Consider the polynomial $f(x) = 4x^{4} + 6x^{3} + 2x^{2} + 2003x + 2003^{2}$. Prove that:
a) the local extrema of $f'(x)$ are positive;
b) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has exactly two real roots and find them.

Consider the polynomial $f(x) = 4x^{4} + 6x^{3} + 2x^{2} + 2003x - 2003^{2}$. Prove that:
a) the local extrema of $f'(x)$ are negative;
b) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has exactly two real roots and find them.

Consider the polynomial $f(x) = 4x^{4} + 6x^{3} + 2x^{2} + 2003x - 2003^{2}$. Prove that:
a) the local extrema of $f'(x)$ are positive;
b) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has exactly three real roots and find them.

Let $M$, $N$ and $P$ be points on the sides $AB$, $BC$ and $CA$ of $\triangle ABC$, respectively. The lines through $M$, $N$ and $P$, parallel to $CA$, $AB$ and $BC$, respectively, meet at a point $T$. Prove that:
a) if $\frac{AM}{MB} = \frac{BN}{NC} = \frac{CP}{PA}$, then $T$ is the centroid of $\triangle ABC$;
b) $S_{MNP} \leq \frac{1}{3} S_{ABC}$.

Let $M$, $N$ and $P$ be points on the sides $AB$, $BC$ and $CA$ of $\triangle ABC$, respectively. The lines through $M$, $N$ and $P$, parallel to $BC$, $AC$ and $AB$, respectively, meet at a point $T$. Prove that:
a) if $\frac{AM}{MB} = \frac{BN}{NC} = \frac{CP}{PA} = 2$, then $T$ is the centroid of $\triangle ABC$;
b) $S_{MNP} \leq \frac{1}{3} S_{ABC}$.

Let $M$, $N$ and $P$ be points on the sides $AB$, $BC$ and $CA$ of $\triangle ABC$, respectively. The lines through $M$, $N$ and $P$, parallel to $BC$, $AC$ and $AB$, respectively, meet at a point $T$. Prove that:
a) if $\frac{AM}{MB} = \frac{BN}{NC} = \frac{CP}{PA}$, then $T$ is the orthocenter of $\triangle ABC$;
b) $S_{MNP} \leq \frac{1}{3} S_{ABC}$.

Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the set of real numbers. Find all $a>0$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ with the following two properties:

a) $f(x)=a x + 1 - a$ for any $x \in [2,3)$;

b) $f(f(x))=2 - 3x$ for any $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the set of real numbers. Find all $a>0$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ with the following two properties:

a) $f(x)=a x + 1 + a$ for any $x \in [2,3)$;

b) $f(f(x))=3 - 2x$ for any $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the set of real numbers. Find all $a>0$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ with the following two properties:

a) $f(x)=a x^2 + 1 - a$ for any $x \in [2,3)$;

b) $f(f(x))=3 - 2x$ for any $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

positive_text:
Problem:
Find all $k > 0$ such that there exists a function $f: [0, 1] \times [0, 1] \to [0, 1]$ that satisfies the following conditions:

a) $f(f(x, y) , z) = f(x, f(y, z) ) $;
b) $f(x, y) = f(y, x) $;
c) $f(x, 1) = x$;
d) $f(z x, z y) = z^{k} f(x, y) $,

for all $x, y, z \in [0, 1]$.

negatives.text:
Let $f(x) = x^{2} - a x + a^{2} - 4$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all $a$, for which:
a) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has two real roots $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ such that $|x_{1}^{3} + x_{2}^{3}| \leq 4$;
b) the inequality $f(x) \geq 0$ holds for all integers $x$.

Let $f(x) = x^{2} - a x + a^{2} - 4$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all $a$, for which:
a) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has two real roots $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ such that $|x_{1}^{3} - x_{2}^{3}| \leq 4$;
b) the inequality $f(x) < 0$ holds for all integers $x$.

Let $f(x) = x^{2} - a x + a^{2} - 4$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all $a$, for which:
a) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has two real roots $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ such that $|x_{1}^{3} - x_{2}^{3}| \leq 4$;
b) the inequality $f(x) \geq 0$ holds for all real numbers $x$.

Let $e_{1} > 0$ and $e_{n+1} = e_{n} - \frac{n}{e_{n}}$ for $n \geq 1$. Prove that:
a) $e_{n} \geq n$ for $n \geq 2$;
b) the sequence $\left\{\frac{e_{n}}{n}\right\}_{n \geq 1}$ converges and find its limit.

Let $f_{1} > 0$ and $f_{n+1} = f_{n} + \frac{n^2}{f_{n}}$ for $n \geq 1$. Prove that:
a) $f_{n} \geq n$ for $n \geq 2$;
b) the sequence $\left\{\frac{f_{n}}{n}\right\}_{n \geq 1}$ converges and find its limit.

Let $g_{1} > 0$ and $g_{n+1} = g_{n} + \frac{1}{g_{n}}$ for $n \geq 1$. Prove that:
a) $g_{n} \geq n$ for $n \geq 2$;
b) the sequence $\left\{\frac{g_{n}}{n}\right\}_{n \geq 1}$ converges and find its limit.

In every cell of an $n \times n$ table one of the numbers $-1, 0$ and $1$ is written. Is it possible the sums of the numbers in every row and every column to be $2n$ non-negative distinct numbers, for:
a) $n=4$;
b) $n=5$?

The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with first term $m$ and difference $2$ is equal to the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric progression with first term $n$ and ratio $\frac{1}{2}$.

a) Prove that $m+n=2^m$;

b) Find $m$ and $n$, if the third term of the geometric progression is equal to the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic progression.

The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with first term $m$ and difference $2$ is equal to the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric progression with first term $n$ and ratio $2$.

a) Prove that $m+n=2^m$;

b) Find $m$ and $n$, if the third term of the geometric progression is equal to the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic progression. Additionally, assume $m < 10$.

The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with first term $m$ and difference $2$ is equal to the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric progression with first term $n$ and ratio $2$.

a) Prove that $m+n=2^m$;

b) Find $m$ and $n$, if the third term of the geometric progression is less than the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic progression.

Let $M$ and $N$ be arbitrary points on the side $AB$ of triangle $ABC$ such that $M$ lies between $A$ and $N$. The line through $M$ parallel to $AC$ meets the circumcircle of $\triangle MNC$ at point $P$, and the line through $M$ parallel to $BC$ meets the circumcircle of $\triangle AMC$ at point $Q$. The line through $N$ parallel to $BC$ meets the circumcircle of $\triangle MNC$ at point $K$, and the line through $N$ parallel to $MC$ meets the circumcircle of $\triangle BNC$ at point $L$. Prove that:

a) the points $P$, $Q$ and $C$ are collinear;

b) the points $P$, $Q$, $K$ and $L$ are concyclic if and only if $AM = BN$.

Let $M$ and $N$ be arbitrary points on the side $AB$ of triangle $ABC$ such that $M$ lies between $A$ and $N$. The line through $M$ parallel to $AC$ meets the circumcircle of $\triangle MNC$ at point $P$, and the line through $M$ parallel to $NC$ meets the circumcircle of $\triangle AMC$ at point $Q$. The line through $N$ parallel to $BC$ meets the circumcircle of $\triangle MNC$ at point $K$, and the line through $N$ parallel to $MC$ meets the circumcircle of $\triangle BNC$ at point $L$. Prove that:

a) the points $P$, $Q$ and $C$ are collinear;

b) the points $P$, $Q$, $K$ and $L$ are concyclic if and only if $AM = 2BN$.

Given sequences $\left(a_{n}\right)$ and $\left(b_{n}\right)$ with recurrence relations $a_{n+1}=2 b_{n}-a_{n}$ and $b_{n+1}=2 a_{n}-b_{n}$, prove that:
a) $a_{n+1}=2\left(a_{1}+b_{1}\right)-a_{n}$;
b) if $a_{n}>0$ for all $n$, then $a_{1}=b_{1}$.

Given sequences $\left(a_{n}\right)$ and $\left(b_{n}\right)$ with recurrence relations $a_{n+1}=2 b_{n}-a_{n}$ and $b_{n+1}=2 a_{n}+b_{n}$, prove that:
a) $a_{n+1}=2\left(a_{1}+b_{1}\right)-3 a_{n}$;
b) if $a_{n}>0$ for all $n$, then $a_{1}=b_{1}$.

positive_text:
Problem:

Let $ABCD$ be a parallelogram. The points $E$ and $F$ of the sides $AB$ and $BC$, respectively, are such that $DE$ is the bisector of $\angle ADF$ and $AE + CF = DF$. The line through $C$ and perpendicular to $DE$ meets the side $AD$ to $L$ and the diagonal $BD$ to $H$. Set $N = DE \cap AC$. To prove it:

a) $AE = DL$;

b) $BC = CD$ if $HN \parallel AD$;

(c) $ABCD$ is a square if $HN \parallel AD$.

negatives.text:
Let $x_{1} = 2$ and $x_{n+1} = 1 - a x_{n}$ for $n \geq 1$. Find all real values of $a$ for which:

a) the sequence $\{x_{n}\}$ is an arithmetic progression;

b) the sequence converges and compute its limit.

Let $x_{1} = 2$ and $x_{n+1} = 1 + a x_{n}^2$ for $n \geq 1$. Find all real values of $a$ for which:

a) the sequence $\{x_{n}\}$ is an arithmetic progression;

b) the sequence converges and compute its limit.

Let $x_{1} = 2$ and $x_{n+1} = 1 + a x_{n}$ for $n \geq 1$. Find all real values of $a$ for which:

a) the sequence $\{x_{n}\}$ is a geometric progression;

b) the sequence converges and compute its limit.

positive_text:
An integer is written in each field of a square table $9 \times 9$. For each $k$ number in the same row (column) , their sum is in the same row (column) . Find the smallest possible number of zeros in the table if:

a) $k = 5$;

b) $k = 8$.

negatives.text:
An integer is written in each of the fields of a $9 \times 9$ square table. For every $k$ numbers in the same row (column), their product is in the same row (column). Find the smallest possible number of zeros in the table if:
a) $k = 5$;
b) $k = 8$.

An integer is written in each of the fields of a $9 \times 9$ square table. For every $k$ numbers in the same row (column), their sum is in the same row (column). Find the smallest possible number of zero entries in the table if:
a) $k = 4$;
b) $k = 9$.

An integer is written in each of the fields of a $9 \times 9$ square table. For every $k$ numbers in the same row (column), their sum is in the same row (column). Find the smallest possible number of zero entries in the table if:
a) $k = 5$;
b) $k = 7$.

positive_text:
Problem:

If $f$ is a function having the following properties:
1) $f(n) $ is defined for each positive integer $n$;
2) $f(n) $ is an integer;
3) $f(2) =2$;
4) $f(m n) =f(m) f(n) $ for all $m$ and $n$;
5) $f(m) >f(n) $ whenever $m>n$.
Try this $f(n) =n$.

negatives.text:
Let $f$ be a function with the following properties:
1) $f(n)$ is defined for every positive integer $n$;
2) $f(n)$ is an integer;
3) $f(2) = 2$;
4) $f(mn) = f(m) + f(n)$ for all $m$ and $n$;
5) $f(m) > f(n)$ whenever $m > n$.
Prove that $f(n) = n$.

Let $f$ be a function with the following properties:
1) $f(n)$ is defined for every positive integer $n$;
2) $f(n)$ is an integer;
3) $f(2) = 3$;
4) $f(mn) = f(m)f(n)$ for all $m$ and $n$;
5) $f(m) > f(n)$ whenever $m > n$.
Prove that $f(n) = n$.

\textbf{Problem:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Solve the simultaneous inequalities, $x > \frac{1}{4x}$ and $x < 0$; i.e., find a single inequality equivalent to the two given simultaneous inequalities.
\item What is the greatest integer which satisfies both inequalities $4x + 13 < 0$ and $x^2 - 3x > 16$?
\item Give a rational number between $\frac{11}{24}$ and $\frac{6}{13}$.
\item Express $100000$ as a product of two integers neither of which is an integral multiple of $10$.
\item Without the use of logarithm tables evaluate
$$\frac{1}{\log_{2} 36} + \frac{1}{\log_{3} 36}$$
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Problem:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Solve the simultaneous inequalities, $x < \frac{1}{4x}$ and $x > 0$; i.e., find a single inequality equivalent to the two given simultaneous inequalities.
\item What is the greatest integer which satisfies both inequalities $4x + 13 > 0$ and $x^2 + 3x > 16$?
\item Give a rational number between $\frac{11}{24}$ and $\frac{6}{13}$.
\item Express $100000$ as a product of two integers neither of which is an integral multiple of $10$.
\item Without the use of logarithm tables evaluate
$$\frac{1}{\log_{2} 36} + \frac{1}{\log_{3} 36}$$
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Problem:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Solve the simultaneous inequalities, $x < \frac{1}{4x}$ and $x < 0$; i.e., find a single inequality equivalent to the two given simultaneous inequalities.
\item What is the greatest integer which satisfies both inequalities $4x + 13 < 0$ and $x^2 + 3x > 16$?
\item Give a rational number between $\frac{11}{24}$ and $\frac{6}{13}$.
\item Express $100000$ as a product of two integers both of which are an integral multiple of $10$.
\item Without the use of logarithm tables evaluate
$$\frac{1}{\log_{2} 36} + \frac{1}{\log_{3} 36}$$
\end{enumerate}

\text{Problem:}
\text{i) If } x = \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^n \text{ and } y = \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^{n+1}, \text{ show that } x^y = y^x + 1.
\text{ii) Show that, for all positive integers } n,
\begin{align*}
1^2 - 2^2 + 3^2 - 4^2 + \cdots + (-1)^n (n-1)^2 + (-1)^{n+1} n^2 = (-1)^n (1 + 2 + \cdots + n)
\end{align*}

\text{Problem:}
\text{i) If } x = \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^n \text{ and } y = \left(1 + \frac{1}{n+1}\right)^{n+1}, \text{ show that } y^x = x^y.
\text{ii) Show that, for all positive integers } n,
\begin{align*}
1^2 - 2^2 + 3^2 - 4^2 + \cdots + (-1)^n (n-1)^2 + (-1)^{n+1} n^2 = (-1)^{n+1} (1 + 2 + \cdots + n)^2
\end{align*}

\text{Problem:}
\text{i) If } x = \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^n \text{ and } y = \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^{n+1}, \text{ show that } y^x = x^y + 1.
\text{ii) Show that, for all positive integers } n,
\begin{align*}
1^2 - 2^2 + 3^2 - 4^2 + \cdots + (-1)^n (n-1)^2 + (-1)^{n+1} n^2 = (-1)^{n+1} (1^2 + 2^2 + \cdots + n^2)
\end{align*}

\text{Let } S \text{ be a set of } n \geq 3 \text{ points in the interior of a circle.}
\text{a) Show that there are three distinct points } a, b, c \in S \text{ and three distinct points } A, B, C \text{ on the circle such that } a \text{ is (strictly) farther from } A \text{ than any other point in } S, b \text{ is farther from } B \text{ than any other point in } S \text{ and } c \text{ is farther from } C \text{ than any other point in } S.
\text{b) Show that for no value of } n \text{ can four such points in } S \text{ (and corresponding points on the circle) be guaranteed.}

\text{Let } S \text{ be a set of } n \geq 3 \text{ points in the interior of a circle.}
\text{a) Show that there are three distinct points } a, b, c \in S \text{ and three distinct points } A, B, C \text{ on the circle such that } a \text{ is (strictly) closer to } A \text{ than any other point in } S, b \text{ is closer to } B \text{ than any other point in } S \text{ and } c \text{ is closer to } C \text{ than any other point in } S, \text{ and } a, b, c \text{ are colinear.}
\text{b) Show that for no value of } n \text{ can four such points in } S \text{ (and corresponding points on the circle) be guaranteed.}

\text{Let } S \text{ be a set of } n \geq 3 \text{ points in the interior of a circle.}
\text{a) Show that there are three distinct points } a, b, c \in S \text{ and three distinct points } A, B, C \text{ on the circle such that } a \text{ is (strictly) closer to } A \text{ than any other point in } S, b \text{ is closer to } B \text{ than any other point in } S \text{ and } c \text{ is closer to } C \text{ than any other point in } S, \text{ and } a, b, c \text{ are all equal.}
\text{b) Show that for no value of } n \text{ can four such points in } S \text{ (and corresponding points on the circle) be guaranteed.}

Let $\triangle ABC$ be a triangle. Points $D$ and $E$ lie on $AB$ and $AC$, and point $F$ lies on $DE$. Let $\frac{AD}{AB} = x$, $\frac{AE}{AC} = y$, $\frac{DF}{DE} = z$. Prove that

(1) $S_{\triangle BDF} = (1-x)y z S_{\triangle ABC}$ and $S_{\triangle CEF} = x(1-y)(1-z) S_{\triangle ABC}$;

(2) $\sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle BDF}} - \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle CEF}} \le \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle ABC}}$.

