positive_text:
Suppose $m$ and $k$ are nonnegative integers, and $p = 2^{2^m} + 1$ is a prime number. Give it a try!

a. $2^{2^{m+1}} p^k \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$;

b. $2^{m+1} p^k$ is the smallest positive integer $n$ satisfying the congruence equation $2^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$.

negatives.text:
Suppose $p = 2^{2^m} + 1$ is prime and $k$ is a non-negative integer. Prove that

a. $2^{2^{m+1}} p^k \equiv -1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$;

b. $2^{m+1} p^k$ is the smallest $n$ such that $2^n \equiv -1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$.

Let $p = 2^{2^m} + 1$ be prime and $k$ a non-negative integer. Prove that

a. $2^{2^{m+1}} p^k \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$;

b. $2^{m+1} + p^k$ is the smallest $n$ such that $2^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$.

Let $p = 2^{2^m} + 1$ be prime and $k$ a non-negative integer. Prove that

a. $2^{2^{m+1}} p^k \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$;

b. $2^{m+1} p^k$ is the smallest $n$ such that $2^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+2}}$.

positive_text:
Find all integers $n \ge 3$ such that one can write a number (not necessarily an integer) in each vertex of a regular gon $n$ -gon in such a way that the following two conditions are met:
(1) Whenever three consecutive vertices of the $n$ -gon, taken clockwise, contain numbers $x, y$ and $z$, respectively, the equation $x = |y - z|$ is valid;
(2) The sum of the numbers in all vertices of the $n$ -gon is 1.

Problem:
Find all lists $\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{2020}\right) $ of non-negative real numbers in order to satisfy all three of the following conditions:
(i) $x_{1} \leq x_{2} \leq \ldots \leq x_{2020}$;
(ii) $x_{2020} \leq x_{1}+1$;
(iii) there is a permutation $\left(y_{1}, y_{2}, \ldots, y_{2020}\right) $ of $\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{2020}\right) $ such that
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{2020}\left(\left(x_{i}+1\right) \left(y_{i}+1\right) \right) ^{2}=8 \sum_{i=1}^{2020} x_{i}^{3}
$$
A list permutation is a list of the same length, with the same entries, but the entries are allowed to be in any order. For example, $(2, 1, 2) $ is a permutation of $(1, 2, 2) $, and they are both permutations of $(2, 2, 1) $. Note that any list is a permutation of itself.

negatives.text:
Find all lists $\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{2020}\right)$ of non-negative real numbers such that the following three conditions are all satisfied:
(i) $x_{1} \leq x_{2} \leq \ldots \leq x_{2020}$;
(ii) $x_{2020} \leq x_{1}+1$;
(iii) there is a permutation $\left(y_{1}, y_{2}, \ldots, y_{2020}\right)$ of $\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{2020}\right)$ such that
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{2020}\left(\left(x_{i}+1\right)\left(y_{i}+1\right)\right)^{2} = 8 \sum_{i=1}^{2020} x_{i}^{2}
$$

Find all lists $\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{2020}\right)$ of non-negative real numbers such that the following three conditions are all satisfied:
(i) $x_{1} \leq x_{2} \leq \ldots \leq x_{2020}$;
(ii) $x_{2020} \leq x_{1}+1$;
(iii) there is a permutation $\left(y_{1}, y_{2}, \ldots, y_{2020}\right)$ of $\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{2020}\right)$ such that
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{2020}\left(\left(x_{i}+1\right)\left(y_{i}+1\right)\right)^{2} \leq 8 \sum_{i=1}^{2020} x_{i}^{3}
$$

Find all lists $\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{2020}\right)$ of non-negative real numbers such that the following three conditions are all satisfied:
(i) $x_{1} \geq x_{2} \geq \ldots \geq x_{2020}$;
(ii) $x_{2020} \leq x_{1}+1$;
(iii) there is a permutation $\left(y_{1}, y_{2}, \ldots, y_{2020}\right)$ of $\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{2020}\right)$ such that
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{2020}\left(\left(x_{i}+1\right)\left(y_{i}+1\right)\right)^{2} = 8 \sum_{i=1}^{2020} x_{i}^{3}
$$

Soit $k \geq 3$ un entier. On définit la suite $\left(a_{n}\right)_{n \geq k}$ par $a_{k}=2k$, et
$$
a_{n} = 
\begin{cases}
a_{n-1}+1 & \text{ si } \operatorname{pgcd}(a_{n-1}, n) = 1 \\
2n-1 & \text{ sinon. }
\end{cases}
$$

Montrer que la suite $\left(a_{n+1} - a_{n}\right)_{n \geq k}$ a une infinité de termes qui sont des nombres premiers.

