Find at least two possible values for $\left(a_{1992}, b_{1992}\right)$.

Let $R$ be the circumradius of triangle $ABC$ and $a, b, c$ its sides. Let $r_{a}, r_{b}, r_{c}$ be the exradii opposite $A, B, C$. If $2R \leq r_{a}$, prove that
(i) $a \geq b$ and $a \geq c$;
(ii) $2R > r_{b}$ and $2R > r_{c}$.

Let $R$ be the circumradius of triangle $ABC$ and $a, b, c$ its sides. Let $r_{a}, r_{b}, r_{c}$ be the exradii opposite $A, B, C$. If $2R \leq r_{a}$, prove that
(i) $a > b$ and $a > c$;
(ii) $2R \geq r_{b}$ and $2R \geq r_{c}$.

Let $x_{1}$ be a positive integer and define $x_{n}$ for $n \geq 2$ by subtracting a nonzero digit of $x_{n-1}$ from $x_{n-1}$. Prove that
(a) the sequence contains an even number;
(b) the sequence contains infinitely many even numbers.

Let $x_{1}$ be a positive integer and define $x_{n}$ for $n \geq 2$ by adding a nonzero digit of $x_{n-1}$ to $x_{n-1}$. Prove that
(a) the sequence contains a number divisible by 3;
(b) the sequence contains infinitely many numbers divisible by 3.

Let $x_{1}$ be a positive integer and define $x_{n}$ for $n \geq 2$ by adding a digit of $x_{n-1}$ (possibly zero) to $x_{n-1}$. Prove that
(a) the sequence contains an even number;
(b) the sequence contains infinitely many even numbers.

positive_text:
Problem:

The function $f$ must be defined from the set $\{(x, y) : x, y$ of real numbers, $x y \neq 0\}$ in the set of all positive real numbers such as
(i) $\quad f(x y, z) =f(x, z) f(y, z) $, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad f(x, y z) =f(x, y) f(x, z) $, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad f(x, 1-x) =1$, for all $x \neq 0, 1$.
Give it a try!
(a) $\quad f(x, x) =f(x, -x) =1$, for all $x \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad f(x, y) f(y, x) =1$, for all $x, y \neq 0$.

negatives.text:
Let $f$ be a function defined from the set $\{(x, y): x, y$ reals, $x y \neq 0\}$ into the set of all positive real numbers such that
(i) $\quad f(x y, z)=f(x, z) f(y, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad f(x, y z)=f(x, y) f(x, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad f(x, 1-x)=1$, for all $x \neq 0,1$.
Prove that
(a) $\quad f(x, x)=f(x,-x)=1$, for all $x \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad f(x, y) f(y, x)=0$, for all $x, y \neq 0$.

Let $f$ be a function defined from the set $\{(x, y): x, y$ reals, $x y \neq 0\}$ into the set of all positive real numbers such that
(i) $\quad f(x y, z)=f(x, z) + f(y, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad f(x, y z)=f(x, y) + f(x, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad f(x, 1-x)=1$, for all $x \neq 0,1$.
Prove that
(a) $\quad f(x, x)=f(x,-x)=1$, for all $x \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad f(x, y) f(y, x)=1$, for all $x, y \neq 0$.

Let $f$ be a function defined from the set $\{(x, y): x, y$ reals, $x y \neq 0\}$ into the set of all positive real numbers such that
(i) $\quad f(x y, z)=f(x, z) f(y, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad f(x, y z)=f(x, y) f(x, z)$, for all $x, y \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad f(x, 1+x)=1$, for all $x \neq 0,1$.
Prove that
